      "tools_when_to_use_what": "Tools — when to use what",
      "top_dollar_willing_to_be_away_from_hom": "Top dollar, willing to be away from home for it",
      "total_material_markup_labor_hr_rate_ov": "Total = (Material × markup) + (Labor hr × rate) + Overhead + Profit",
      "touch_swipe_sideways_tilt_buttons": "👆 Touch: swipe sideways to orbit, ▲▼ to tilt",
      "trade_culture_historically_still_inclu": "Trade culture historically + still includes drinking, opioid use (often from work-related injuries → pain pills). Risk factor for transitioning off pain meds → alcohol → harder substances. Be aware. Many shops drug-test (especially safety-sensitive work like BIW). Maine SAMHSA helpline: 1-800-499-0027. Free, confidential.",
      "trade_offs": "⚖ Trade-offs",
      "trade_school_community_college": "Trade school / community college",
      "traditional_welding_robots_200k_turn_k": "Traditional welding robots: ~$200K turn-key cell, weeks of programming, safety cage, requires dedicated programmer. Cobots: ~$50K turn-key, hours of programming, no cage needed, programmed by existing welder. Small shops can now afford automation that was previously out of reach. Result: more shops automating, but cobots typically supplement (not replace) skilled manual welders.",
      "travel_angle_continuously_changes_as_b": "Travel angle continuously changes as bead progresses around. Body position changes too.",
      "travel_speed": "Travel Speed",
      "travel_speed_2": "Travel Speed",
      "travel_too_fast": "Travel too fast",
      "travel_too_slow": "Travel too slow",
      "travels_to_customer_sites_for_installa": "Travels to customer sites for installation + commissioning + troubleshooting of robot welding cells. FANUC, ABB, Lincoln Electric, etc.",
      "triangle_fillet_square_square_groove_v": "Triangle = fillet, square = square groove, \"V\" = V groove, half-V = bevel groove, U = U groove, circle = plug/slot.",
      "triangle_types_you_cut": "Triangle types you cut",
      "trigonometry_basics_sin_cos_tan_for_an": "• Trigonometry basics (sin/cos/tan for angled cuts)",
      "trigonometry_when_you_need_it": "Trigonometry — when you need it",
      "triple_deoxidized_al_ti_zr_added_most_": "Triple-deoxidized (Al, Ti, Zr added). Most clean MIG wire. Best for: contaminated/rusty base metal, primer-coated steel. Often used by farm + repair welders.",
      "true_color_1_1_1_1_or_1_1_1_2_optical_": "True-color (1/1/1/1 or 1/1/1/2 optical class) = much easier to see puddle + edges. Worth the upgrade.",
      "true_color_vs_amber": "TRUE COLOR vs amber: ",
      "try_next": "→ Try next: ",
      "tubular_structures": "Tubular Structures",
      "tubular_wire_with_flux_inside_two_type": "Tubular wire with flux inside. Two types: self-shielded (E71T-11) needs no gas; gas-shielded (E70T-1) uses CO2.",
      "tungsten_electrode_non_consumable_sepa": "Tungsten electrode (non-consumable) + separate filler rod (ER70S-2/3/6, ER308 for stainless, ER4043/5356 for aluminum) + shielding gas (100% argon usually).",
      "tungsten_electrodes_color_coded_tip": "Tungsten electrodes — color-coded tip!",
      "tungsten_preparation": "💡 Tungsten preparation",
      "two_distinct_failure_modes": "Two distinct failure modes",
      "two_pieces_lying_parallel_with_their_e": "Two pieces lying parallel, with their edges aligned and welded along that edge. Used for sealing thin-gauge sheet metal where strength is secondary.",
      "two_pieces_meeting_at_right_angles_at_": "Two pieces meeting at right angles, at their edges (not perpendicular through the middle like a T-joint). Forms an L-shape. Common in box fabrication.",
      "two_pieces_meeting_perpendicular_formi": "Two pieces meeting perpendicular, forming a T-shape. The \"fillet weld\" you see on furniture, brackets, gussets, and any structural intersection.",
      "two_pieces_of_metal_lying_in_the_same_": "Two pieces of metal lying in the same plane, edge-to-edge. The most common joint in pressure vessels, pipe, ship hull plate, and any application where the two pieces must form one continuous surface.",
      "two_source_types": "🔧 Two source types",
      "typical_20_35_cfh_cubic_feet_hour_for_": "Typical: 20-35 CFH (cubic feet/hour) for MIG. 15-25 CFH for TIG. Too low = porosity. Too high = turbulent flow that SUCKS atmosphere in = porosity. Both extremes cause same defect. Sweet spot is your machine's recommended range adjusted for breeze + nozzle distance.",
      "typical_entry": "Typical entry: ",
      "typical_mig_18_26v_tig_12_22v_stick_18": "Typical: MIG 18-26V, TIG 12-22V, Stick 18-30V",
      "typical_shop_noise_levels": "Typical shop noise levels",
      "tyrone_j": "Tyrone J.",
      "u_s_navy_hull_maintenance_technician_h": "U.S. Navy Hull Maintenance Technician (HT) rate is essentially a paid welding career — Navy pays for training, gives you 4-6 years of experience welding on ships. After service, BIW, Portsmouth Naval, and civilian shipyards actively recruit Navy HTs.",
      "ua_local_716_pipefitter_apprenticeship": "UA Local 716 Pipefitter Apprenticeship",
      "ultrasonic_testing_ut": "📡 Ultrasonic Testing (UT)",
      "ultrasonic_ut": "📡 Ultrasonic (UT)",
      "undercut": "Undercut",
      "undercut_2": "Undercut",
      "underpricing_maine_welders_chronically": "Underpricing (Maine welders chronically underbid each other). No quoting discipline (verbal estimates that become disputes). Equipment failure (one fried machine = month of lost income if no backup). Cash flow gaps (some customers pay 60-90 days). Don't take on jobs you don't have the right equipment for. Don't weld something safety-critical without the qualifications.",
      "underwater_welding": "Underwater Welding",
      "underwater_welding_2": "Underwater Welding",
      "underwater_welding_fatality_rate_is_am": "Underwater welding fatality rate is among the highest in any trade.",
